"Solutions for Advent of Code 2025, Day 03.

Part 1: Pick exactly two batteries from each bank to form the largest
two-digit joltage. Sum the maximums across all banks.

Part 2: Pick exactly twelve batteries to form the largest twelve-digit
joltage. Sum across all banks.

Approach: greedy selection with a monotonic stack — one pass per bank,
dropping a kept digit whenever a larger one arrives and skips remain.

Notable eucalypt techniques:
- `;` forward compose for point-free pipelines: `batteries ; pick(2) ; digits-to-num`
- `str.code-points` to read digits as character codes
- `foldl-strict` with a tuple accumulator and expression anaphora
  `_0 * 10 + _1` for digit-to-number conversion
- a recursive helper whose tail position is a single `then`
"

` "The digits of a bank as numbers, read off the character codes."
batteries(line): line str.code-points map(- 48)

` "Push digit `d` onto the stack of kept digits (most recent first),
first discarding up to `skips` smaller digits from its top: a smaller
digit before a larger one can always be dropped to improve the number.
Returns `[stack, remaining-skips]`."
push(d, skips, stack): {
  droppable: (skips > 0) ∧ (stack non-nil?) ∧ ((stack head) < d)
}.(droppable then(push(d, skips - 1, stack tail), [cons(d, stack), skips]))

` "Fold step: keep `d`, spending the skip budget."
keep-step([stack, skips], d): push(d, skips, stack)

` "The largest k-digit number formed by k of the digits in order.  One
pass with a monotonic stack: each of the `n - k` skips is spent on the
first smaller digit it can improve, and any surplus drops off the end."
pick(k, ds): {
  kept: ds foldl-strict(keep-step, [[], (ds count) - k]) first
}.(kept reverse take(k))

` "Convert a list of digits to a number: [9,8,7] -> 987."
digits-to-num(ds): ds foldl-strict(_0 * 10 + _1, 0)

solve(data, n): data map(batteries ; pick(n) ; digits-to-num) sum

` { target: :test
    import: "ex1=inputs/day03-ex1.txt" }
test: {
  part-1: solve(ex1, 2) //= 357
  part-2: solve(ex1, 12) //= 3121910778619
  RESULT: (part-1 ∧ part-2) then(:PASS, :FAIL)
}
